# MOBILITY

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Mobility model defines how joint mobility, soft tissue extensibility and motor control contribute to athletic performance.

Mobility is not flexibility alone.

Mobility is the ability to actively control movement throughout the required range of motion.

The Combat Athlete System develops mobility to improve performance, movement quality and resilience.

---

# Definition

Mobility is the capacity to actively move a joint through its functional range of motion while maintaining stability, coordination and control.

Mobility combines:

Joint Range of Motion

↓

Motor Control

↓

Strength

↓

Coordination

↓

Movement Quality

All components are required.

---

# Philosophy

The objective of mobility training is not to maximize joint range.

The objective is to obtain the range required for efficient and safe movement.

More mobility is not always better.

Appropriate mobility is.

---

# Biological Foundations

Mobility depends upon:

Joint Structure

Muscle Length

Tendon Properties

Connective Tissue

Neural Regulation

Motor Control

Strength

Pain

No single tissue determines mobility.

---

# Mobility vs Flexibility

CAS distinguishes:

Flexibility

↓

Passive range of motion.

Mobility

↓

Active, controlled range of motion.

Combat performance depends primarily on mobility.

---

# Active Mobility

Examples

Deep squat position.

High kick.

Technical stand-up.

Guard retention.

Lunge.

The athlete actively controls the movement.

---

# Passive Mobility

Examples

Static stretching.

Assisted stretching.

Passive flexibility does not automatically transfer to sport performance.

---

# Joint Mobility

CAS evaluates mobility by joint.

Ankle

Knee

Hip

Lumbar Spine

Thoracic Spine

Shoulder

Elbow

Wrist

Neck

Each joint has different functional requirements.

---

# Functional Mobility

Mobility should always be evaluated within movement.

Examples

Squat.

Sprint.

Kick.

Throw.

Punch.

Sprawl.

Bridge.

Mobility without functional application has limited value.

---

# Combat Sports Mobility

Combat athletes require mobility for:

Deep level changes.

High kicks.

Guard work.

Ground transitions.

Throwing mechanics.

Punching mechanics.

Footwork.

Mobility requirements differ between combat sports.

---

# Stability Relationship

Mobility and stability are complementary.

High mobility without stability reduces performance.

High stability without mobility limits movement.

The objective is controlled movement.

---

# Programming Variables

Mobility development depends on:

Frequency

Duration

Intensity

Movement Quality

Specificity

Consistency

Mobility responds well to frequent exposure.

---

# Types of Mobility Training

CAS recognizes multiple methods.

Dynamic Mobility

Active Mobility

Loaded Mobility

Controlled Articular Rotations

PNF

Static Stretching

Movement Preparation

Sport-Specific Mobility

Each method serves different objectives.

---

# Dynamic Mobility

Primary use

Warm-up.

Movement preparation.

Neural activation.

Examples

Leg swings.

Arm circles.

Walking lunges.

Hip openers.

---

# Loaded Mobility

Mobility developed under resistance.

Examples

Goblet squat holds.

Jefferson curls.

Cossack squats.

Split squats.

Loaded mobility improves usable range.

---

# Static Stretching

Primarily used:

Post-training.

Recovery.

Specific mobility deficits.

Static stretching is one tool,

not the foundation of mobility.

---

# Motor Control

Mobility requires movement control.

Examples

Hip control.

Scapular control.

Core control.

Breathing.

Without motor control,

new ranges cannot be used effectively.

---

# Common Mobility Limitations

Examples

Ankle dorsiflexion.

Hip internal rotation.

Thoracic extension.

Shoulder flexion.

Thoracic rotation.

These limitations influence exercise selection.

---

# Fatigue

Mobility decreases under fatigue.

Poor mobility may also increase fatigue by reducing movement efficiency.

Both relationships should be considered.

---

# Recovery

Mobility work may contribute to:

Recovery.

Movement quality.

Pain reduction.

Preparation.

Recovery benefits remain individual.

---

# Assessment

Possible assessments include:

Deep Squat.

Overhead Squat.

Hip Rotation.

Shoulder Flexion.

Ankle Dorsiflexion.

Active Straight Leg Raise.

Thoracic Rotation.

Movement Screens.

Assessment should always remain functional.

---

# Transfer to Combat Sports

Mobility contributes directly to:

Kick Height.

Punch Mechanics.

Throwing Efficiency.

Ground Mobility.

Guard Retention.

Footwork.

Level Changes.

Mobility improves movement options.

---

# Interaction With Other Qualities

Mobility supports:

Strength.

Power.

Speed.

Technique.

Recovery.

Stability.

Movement Quality.

Mobility should never be trained in isolation.

